  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21197v2</id>
    <title>Supernovae Driven Winds Impede Lyman Continuum Escape from Dwarf Galaxies in First 10 Myr</title>
    <updated>2026-07-07T16:56:20Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21197v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.21197v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Observations suggest that UV-bright, compact star-forming galaxies produce enough ionizing (Lyman continuum; LyC) photons to reionize the Universe. Yet, the efficiency of LyC escape and the roles of radiation, stellar winds, and supernovae remain uncertain. Using medium-resolution spectra of six nearly identical local star-forming galaxies, we directly trace, for the first time, the evolution of a multiphase wind through individual spectral lines alongside measurements of the LyC escape fraction. We find that LyC escape peaks early, during a period dominated by intense radiation and stellar winds but lacking a fast galactic wind. As the starbursts age, supernovae drive and accelerate the wind, progressively suppressing LyC escape. These results highlight the need for cosmological simulations to incorporate early feedback as a key driver of reionization.</summary>
